 `error` and `reason` carry the same text; the bridge client reads `reason`.
+A rejection carries no claim data in any configuration.
+
+### Other endpoints and plaintext
+
+`GET /inspect/:id` reports which claims a session disclosed (paths) against
+the registered scope; it does not render values. `POST /response/:id` answers
+the wallet with the status, the inspect URL and the redirect. The ZK
+endpoints and the relay endpoints belong to other sessions (`GET /result/:id`
+is 404 for them). No other endpoint of this service returns a disclosed value
+or a presentation, so the token is required on `GET /result/:id` only.
 
 ### Unchanged
 
 `GET /`, `GET /request/:id`, `GET /inspect/:id`, the ZK endpoints
 (`GET /zk/result/:id` included) and the relay endpoints behave as before. The
-only change on the shared response path is that a verified default-path
-session now also keeps its presentation string (in memory, same lifetime as
-the parsed result, gated by the flag).
+change on the shared response path is what a verified default-path session
+keeps: the minimized summary always, the presentation string and the parsed
+view only behind their flags (in memory, same lifetime as the summary).
 
 ## Configuration
 
 | variable | default | effect |
 |---|---|---|
-| `RESULT_INCLUDES_PRESENTATION` | `true` | keep the decrypted presentation of a verified session and serve it from `GET /result/:id`; `false` keeps the parsed summary only |
+| `RESULT_TOKEN` | unset | shared secret for `GET /result/:id`, presented as `X-Result-Token`; required whenever a plaintext flag is on (503 otherwise); when set, required on every read |
+| `RESULT_INCLUDES_PRESENTATION` | `false` | keep the decrypted presentation of a verified session and serve it as `presentation`; the bridge's verifier mode needs `true`; the SP1 route's plaintext boundary |
+| `RESULT_INCLUDES_NAMES` | `false` | keep the parsed PID view with its disclosed values and serve it as `verified_pid` |
+| `BRIDGE_MAX_PENDING` | `1000` | most `POST /request` sessions allowed to wait for a wallet at once; further requests get 429 until one completes or expires |
+| `BRIDGE_SESSION_TTL_SECS` | `900` | seconds after creation at which a `POST /request` session and its outcome are dropped; pruned on the next `POST /request` |
 
-Nothing else is needed: the endpoints are always mounted, the nonce table is
-in memory, and sessions live as long as the process (no pruning, as for the
-relay table and the results map today).
+The bridge's verifier mode therefore runs with `RESULT_INCLUDES_PRESENTATION=true`
+and `RESULT_TOKEN=<secret>` on the verifier, and `VERIFIER_RESULT_TOKEN=<same
+secret>` on the bridge (`nachweis-app/service`, see its README). The endpoints
+are always mounted and the tables are in memory.
 
 ## Limits
 
-- No pruning: the bridge table and the results map grow with the number of
-  sessions until restart. Same as the rest of the service today.
+- Pruning covers the bridge table and the outcomes of bridge sessions only,
+  and runs on `POST /request`. Landing-page outcomes, the relay table and the
+  openid4vp session store still grow until restart; the cap bounds what a
+  caller of `POST /request` can add, not the rest.
 - One outcome per session: a second wallet POST to a completed session is
   refused at the openid4vp session-store layer, and the first outcome stands.
-- `GET /result/:id` is unauthenticated beyond the session id. See the privacy
-  note: same host only.
+- The token is a shared secret between two processes on one host, sent in a
+  header. It is not a substitute for keeping the endpoint off a public
+  origin. See the privacy note.
 - Transport-level failures on the wallet's POST (a malformed form body, a
   session the store refuses) are not recorded as a rejection; the session stays
   `pending` and the wallet may retry. The bridge's own poll timeout (10 min)
-  bounds the wait.
+  bounds the wait; the TTL (15 min) drops the session afterwards.
